WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:06.424
Here on Journey to the Microcosmos, we have
a complicated relationship with worms.

00:00:06.424 --> 00:00:08.492
On the one hand, they are gross.

00:00:08.492 --> 00:00:09.940
They wiggle around.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.797
They end up in body parts.

00:00:11.797 --> 00:00:13.469
They cause disease.

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:15.067
All those things aren't great.

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:18.000
On the other hand, they’re everywhere.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.750
You cannot escape worms, especially in the
microcosmos.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:27.421
And given everything we have said about how
gross worms are, that doesn’t sound like

00:00:27.421 --> 00:00:28.751
good news.

00:00:28.751 --> 00:00:32.000
But there’s something to be said for ubiquity.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.160
Absence may make the heart grow fonder, but
constant worm encounters can go a long way too.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.267
We might even have a little affection for
them at this point.

00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:48.980
So today, we’re going to look back on some of those
many encounters and see what it is that makes

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.724
worms so hard to ignore.

00:00:51.724 --> 00:00:58.029
Let’s start out though by talking 
about what a worm actually even is.

00:01:12.878 --> 00:01:15.810
There are no ghosts in the microcosmos.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.330
There is no Halloween in the microcosmos.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:24.790
But if there were, this flatworm would fit
in quite nicely with its spectral appearance.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:29.590
It might be navigating its way through a field
of other organisms, but it looks like it had

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:34.290
three minutes to prepare for a costume party
and decided to go with the classic “old

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.630
bedsheet with eyeholes” ghost costume.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:45.670
And maybe comparing this flatworm (or even
worms in general) to a ghost is kind of apt.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:52.320
Ghosts straddle two worlds, and so do worms,
though in their case, instead of bridging

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:59.490
the realm of the living and dead, worms manage
to stretch between the micro and the macro.

00:02:12.138 --> 00:02:18.560
And akin to ghosts, worms might seem like
a sort of reduced form of life.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:25.240
After all, for a lot of us, worms are the
wiggly tubes that we dug up from playgrounds.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:31.290
And of all of the worms, flatworms are perhaps
the most simple of all.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:37.680
But even digging into what it means to be
the simplest worm makes the whole notion of

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.390
simplicity seem ludicrous.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:48.900
So let’s start with the broader question:
what are worms, other than wiggly, self-mating

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.650
tubes?

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:56.620
While we generally use the word “worm”
to describe many different, unrelated, invertebrate,

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:02.140
tubular animals that belong to a number of
different phyla, there are three main phyla

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:03.510
that people focus on.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:10.800
The first are our flatworm buddies here, who
are known more formally as Platyhelminthes.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.060
Then there is the phylum Nematoda, known less
formally as roundworms.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:20.470
We’ve come across nematodes many times in
our journey through the microcosmos, which

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:27.069
makes sense given that they are one of the
most abundant animals on this planet.

00:03:27.069 --> 00:03:33.830
And last are the Annelida, phylum of our playground
friend, the earthworm.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:39.980
While earthworms are a bit beyond the scope
of the micro, if you will, we’ve also got

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.349
this little Stylaria lacustris.

00:03:42.349 --> 00:03:49.150
At the tip of its head is the proboscis, an
antenna-like structure that the worm uses

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:50.150
for feeding.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:56.549
And as we scan past the head, you can see
that the length of the Stylaria’s body is

00:03:56.549 --> 00:03:59.340
segmented.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.370
Of the three phyla we’ve mentioned, annelids
are the most complex.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:10.069
They have a closed circulatory system with
tubes that transport nutrients and oxygen

00:04:10.069 --> 00:04:16.260
throughout their body, as well as a complete
digestive system that has both a mouth to

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.140
take in food and an anus to get rid of waste.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:27.780
If we think of worms in general as a gut enclosed
within a body wall, then that gut is a tube

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:34.949
and the body is a tube, and you can imagine
that worms are a tube inside a tube.

00:04:34.949 --> 00:04:40.570
Now what separates these different groups
is the space that separates one of those tubes

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.670
from the other.

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:49.160
Complex systems inside annelids are due to
what’s called a coelom, a tissue-lined cavity

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:55.370
that sits between the tube of the digestive
tract and the tube of the body wall.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:05:01.580
Importantly, this cavity is filled with fluid,
which facilitates the development of organs

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:05.960
along with the transport of nutrients around
the body.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:12.250
The development of the first coelom was a
very big deal because coeloms don’t just

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:15.539
allow annelids to have complex systems.

00:05:15.539 --> 00:05:21.460
They connect the seemingly simple organisms
we’re talking about here to more complex

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.690
animals—like us!

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:29.880
Because annelids and humans are both what
is known as coelomates, or eucoelomates.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.430
We are animals that have coeloms.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:40.300
So, thank you to the ancestor that we share
with earthworms who developed the first coelom,

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:46.030
who allowed more complex organisms like you
to exist.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:50.930
Nematodes, on the other hand, are what are
called pseudocoelomates.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:56.910
They still have a coelom-like fluid-filled
cavity, but the cavity isn’t lined with

00:05:56.910 --> 00:06:00.130
tissue like you find in true coeloms.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:05.300
Other microscopic pseudocoelomates include
gastrotrichs and rotifers.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:12.860
This pseudocoelom is a slight but significant
difference in nematodes when compared to annelids,

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:17.289
corresponding to a slightly less complex body
plan, though nematodes

00:06:17.289 --> 00:06:21.310
still do have a complete digestive system.

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:24.590
The flatworms are the simplest of all.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:28.660
Starting with the coelom: flatworms don’t
actually have one.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.190
They’re considered acoelomates.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:36.500
That area between the gut and body wall where
annelids and nematodes have a cavity full

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:40.569
of fluid is instead full of tissue.

00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:46.440
This has a few consequences for the flatworm,
but one of the biggest ones is that this area

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:51.790
can’t support the development of specialized
systems that you see in other animals.

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:59.639
The lack of coelom imposes limits on the flatworm’s
body and lifestyle, reducing both its complexity

00:06:59.639 --> 00:07:00.639
and size.

00:07:00.639 --> 00:07:05.204
But that does not mean the flatworm lives
a simple life.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:20.349
Of the three worm groups, flatworms are the
most like a tube within another tube, except

00:07:20.349 --> 00:07:24.039
that their outer tube is much less cylindrical.

00:07:24.039 --> 00:07:29.840
The “flat” in “flatworm” is actually
an adaptation: without a circulatory system

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:36.960
or respiratory system, the worm relies on
diffusion across its outer membrane to supply

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:39.610
cells with oxygen.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:46.930
Being flatter gets the cells closer to the
outside and better positioned for diffusion.

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:52.159
And without the ability to build a complete
digestive system like those found in nematodes

00:07:52.159 --> 00:07:59.229
and annelids, flatworms have to adjust to
life without that most under-appreciated body

00:07:59.229 --> 00:08:01.680
part of all: the anus.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:07.960
They take in food through one opening, digest
it in their gut, and then spew the waste back

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:10.479
out from that same opening.

00:08:10.479 --> 00:08:17.720
So unlike other organisms who have separate
entrances and exits for their food, the flatworm

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:22.860
must wait to finish digesting before it can
eat again.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:27.379
But this is not the only way flatworms can
get rid of waste.

00:08:27.379 --> 00:08:34.490
Their body is lined with a special type of
cell called a flame cell that also gets waste

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:35.490
out of the worm.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:42.690
While it may not have the elegance of an interconnected
set of organs, this system is its own form

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.019
of complexity.

00:08:44.019 --> 00:08:53.970
Basically, every flame cell is one single-celled
kidney, allowing the worm to just...ooze waste

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:55.060
from its skin.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:09:01.070
And this is just scratching the surface of
the biological intricacy flatworms are capable

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:02.070
of.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:08.279
Like many of their more involved worm counterparts,
flatworms can reproduce both asexually and

00:09:08.279 --> 00:09:09.279
sexually.

00:09:09.279 --> 00:09:16.200
And when reproducing sexually, they can mate
with another flatworm, or even with themselves--both

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:19.440
making and fertilizing their own eggs.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:25.860
There’s also those flatworm species that
can regenerate, a trick that our more developed

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:29.550
bodies are, alas, not capable of.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:35.468
Even the many weird ways they move make the
word “wiggle” feel reductive.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:56.230
Our perception of simplicity across nature
is built on comparisons.

00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:59.149
Compared to us, an earthworm may seem simple.

00:09:59.149 --> 00:10:05.709
Put that earthworm next to a flatworm, and
suddenly the earthworm represents a gigantic

00:10:05.709 --> 00:10:07.230
biological advancement.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:17.295
But then put that flatworm next to a bacteria,
and suddenly it contains multitudes, it is a universe.

00:10:17.295 --> 00:10:25.833
That flatworm is a marvel, an almost unbelievable
testament to the power of evolution.

00:10:30.096 --> 00:10:33.997
So we started with the simplest worm of all.

00:10:33.997 --> 00:10:38.731
And if you’re someone who is skeptical that
worms can be interesting, then maybe watching

00:10:38.731 --> 00:10:42.899
a simple, jiggly tube was not enough to sway
you.

00:10:42.899 --> 00:10:48.660
But nature has a way of building on simplicity,
creating fascinating creatures out of even

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:50.905
the most basic of blueprints.

00:10:50.905 --> 00:10:53.910
And that brings us to the next worm we’re
going to focus on…

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:58.577
the ones we affectionately call “polka-dotted
vacuum worms.”

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:06.339
We recently did an episode
about nematodes,

00:11:06.339 --> 00:11:11.589
the phylum of worm that outnumbers
just about every animal on this planet.

00:11:11.589 --> 00:11:13.899
Now, it's not the most striking of animals,

00:11:13.899 --> 00:11:17.920
but the nematode has had a few
distinguished scientific decades,

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:21.010
thanks to its many uses in laboratories.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:22.010
So as far as worms go,

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:26.040
the nematode seems to dominate
much of our scientific understanding.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:32.260
But worms, despite their seemingly simple
bodies, are a diverse bunch.

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:37.220
Which is why we thought for today it might
be fun to visit with a less famous worm

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:40.420
and like one of those relatives
that you don't really know very much about

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:44.669
But every time you see them,
there's a new strange story to unpack

00:11:48.649 --> 00:11:53.709
They are the Aeolosomatids,
a family of freshwater worms.

00:11:53.709 --> 00:11:56.350
The ones that you see here are invaders.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.329
They showed up uninvited
in a blepharisma culture that

00:11:59.329 --> 00:12:03.680
James, our master of microscopes,
has been taken care of for a long time.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.420
And while Aeolosoma worms are
you know, worms,

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:11.209
they are in a different class of worm
because as we have seen before,

00:12:11.209 --> 00:12:14.199
there are, in fact, many ways to be a tube.

00:12:14.199 --> 00:12:17.990
Where nematodes are roundworms,
Aeolosoma are segmented,

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:22.399
placing them in the Annelid phylum,
along with earthworms and leeches,

00:12:22.399 --> 00:12:25.950
Aeolosoma are usually several millimeters
in length,

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:29.019
their bodies divided into more
than ten segments that you can see

00:12:29.019 --> 00:12:33.526
scrunching up and expanding as the worm
wiggles its way through the microcosmos.

00:12:35.504 --> 00:12:38.660
The Aeolosoma are striking to look at.

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:42.230
You can see their organs
through their transparent bodies,

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:47.620
and as it moves, bundles of long bristly
hairs wave along the side of its body.

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:51.730
Those hairs mark the Aeolosoma
as a specific type of an annelid

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:54.959
called a polychaete, or bristle worm.

00:12:54.959 --> 00:12:58.690
Some bristle worms are found
in unusual places like hydrothermal vents,

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:02.800
but our Aeolosoma come
from a much more mundane home.

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:06.260
They're usually found in bodies
of fresh water where they'd like to crawl

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:10.120
among the leaves and algae
that settle at the bottom of the water.

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:13.620
And inside their bodies
are colorful gland cells,

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:16.470
though no one is really sure
what those cells exist for

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:19.820
or why they have their particular colors.

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:23.950
And some species, the cells are green
and others they're yellow.

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:26.230
And sometimes,
as with our worms, they're red.

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:28.899
The final result is a worm
that looks a little like it

00:13:28.899 --> 00:13:32.428
ran into a porcupine
while also having caught chicken pox.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:39.889
While there are some Aeolosoma
species that reproduce sexually,

00:13:39.889 --> 00:13:44.390
most reproduce asexually dividing
to form a copy of itself.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:47.589
The Aeolosoma creates its clone at its end,

00:13:47.589 --> 00:13:51.149
linking the old and new versions of itself
like a chain.

00:13:51.149 --> 00:13:54.050
You can see the new Aeolosoma here,

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:57.690
looking like it's attached to the other’s
butt because,

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:59.860
it's attached to the other’s butt.

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:03.020
And this chain can keep going
as the Aeolosoma

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:06.949
keeps dividing, adding more worms
that are connected together

00:14:06.949 --> 00:14:10.019
so that the final length
of their combined bodies sometimes

00:14:10.019 --> 00:14:13.149
reaches around ten millimeters total.

00:14:13.149 --> 00:14:15.110
That's ten millimeters of clones

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:20.720
combined to create one giant mega worm
until eventually the chain breaks

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:22.568
and they all go their separate ways.

00:14:24.634 --> 00:14:26.149
So when James found these worms

00:14:26.149 --> 00:14:29.930
invading his samples, you'd think
maybe this would be an exciting find.

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:34.180
Here is a culture full of bristled,
polka-dotted, chain-forming clones.

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:36.050
What could be more exciting!

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:40.779
Well, as wonderful as they are to
look at these invasions are not ideal

00:14:40.779 --> 00:14:44.690
because they are also essentially vacuum cleaners.

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:48.200
Their mouths are lined with cilia
that wave around and help the worms

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:51.079
suction up bits of plant and animal debris.

00:14:51.079 --> 00:14:52.079
When they're in a pond,

00:14:52.079 --> 00:14:55.779
They like to crawl across leaves
and algae for their meals.

00:14:55.779 --> 00:14:56.779
But when you find them

00:14:56.779 --> 00:15:00.050
in bottles of ciliate cultures,
you've been lovingly maintaining,

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:01.980
that's when things get a bit dicier.

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:03.759
Because Aeolosoma will eat

00:15:03.759 --> 00:15:07.606
just about anything, including each other.

00:15:08.559 --> 00:15:09.399
Indeed.

00:15:09.399 --> 00:15:10.649
in one very dramatic scene

00:15:10.649 --> 00:15:15.829
documented in 1901 scientists
observing the species Aeolosoma tenebrarum

00:15:15.829 --> 00:15:17.959
described the way
these chains of worms

00:15:17.959 --> 00:15:22.350
would twist up in each other,
creating a writhing, tangled ball of worms

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:25.960
that would stay stuck together
for long periods of time.

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:29.030
And when the scientists pulled
these balls apart, they usually found

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:31.829
at least one worm
that had been partially eaten.

00:15:31.829 --> 00:15:35.360
I'm sure the etiquette around
cannibalistic frenzies varies,

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:38.490
but for most animals, getting eaten
by another member of your species

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:41.569
would seem, at the very least,
a little rude.

00:15:41.569 --> 00:15:45.370
But when you're Aeolosoma,
it's not that big of a deal.

00:15:45.370 --> 00:15:47.540
Honestly, it's not much
more than an inconvenience,

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:50.660
because if a part of it gets eaten,
it can always regenerate.

00:15:51.808 --> 00:15:57.069
In one case, the scientists watching these
balls of worms found that one worm had its

00:15:57.069 --> 00:15:58.170
head eaten.

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:02.389
But in about three days
it was able to make a new one.

00:16:02.389 --> 00:16:06.569
It would probably have taken less time
to regenerate other parts of their body-

00:16:06.569 --> 00:16:09.740
heads seemed to take the Aeolosoma a bit longer,

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:12.430
perhaps because of all the complex parts
that need to be rebuilt.

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:17.730
And the Aeolosoma can regenerate even
when it is cut into multiple segments.

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:22.370
This superpower has made one species
called Aeolosoma viride

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:24.259
particularly interesting to scientists.

00:16:24.259 --> 00:16:26.759
And it's not just
that they can regenerate.

00:16:26.759 --> 00:16:30.519
After all, as incredible as this ability
is, there are plenty of other animals

00:16:30.519 --> 00:16:32.370
that can regenerate as well.

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:36.670
But scientists aren't just interested
in how animals regenerate.

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:39.889
They also want to know how
those regenerative abilities

00:16:39.889 --> 00:16:43.060
change as the animal gets older.

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:46.370
That's a difficult question to study
because as you might expect,

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:50.029
self-healing animals have often, pretty long
lifespans.

00:16:50.029 --> 00:16:54.550
So it's a challenge to wait years
or even decades to study how their ability

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:58.880
to regenerate changes
with the wear and tear of aging.

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:02.860
Aeolosoma viride
however, has a lifespan

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:06.750
of only about two months,
which means it goes from young to old

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:11.329
on a manageable timescale for scientists
cycling through experiments.

00:17:11.329 --> 00:17:14.530
And that makes it a useful organism
to observe how that capacity

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:17.919
to rebuild itself changes
as the worm ages.

00:17:17.919 --> 00:17:21.130
But as useful as regeneration
is for survival,

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:24.689
it is not the only tool
the worm relies on.

00:17:24.689 --> 00:17:29.860
After all, not all dangers can easily
be patched up by rebuilding body parts.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:34.010
Sometimes the worm has to preempt
dangerous conditions, and for that

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:35.878
it turns to the cyst.

00:17:39.834 --> 00:17:43.900
In nature, the worms will likely begin
forming these cysts in autumn,

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:49.309
when the water gets cold and begins to fill
with the remains of decomposing life.

00:17:49.309 --> 00:17:53.510
And as the temperatures continue
to fall, the worms begin to slow down,

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:58.510
crawling to areas full of delicious debris
for them to stock up on, and eventually,

00:17:58.510 --> 00:18:01.610
the worms begin to secrete a mucus,
creating a gooey

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:05.299
shell that then hardens into a cyst.

00:18:05.299 --> 00:18:10.990
You can see the granules of red pigment
swirling around as the worm moves inside.

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:11.990
Some of that activity

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:16.220
might be the peristaltic movement
of its intestines, but it's also possible

00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:21.720
that the warmth of the microscope lamp
is causing the worms to stir as well.

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:23.030
And in their ponds,

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:27.090
when warm weather comes, the worm
will get ready to emerge from its encased

00:18:27.090 --> 00:18:31.450
hibernation, using its head
to push at the hardened case of its cyst

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:34.990
until it manages to poke a hole through
from which it can escape.

00:18:34.990 --> 00:18:35.990
It can take a worm anywhere

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.230
from 30 minutes
to several hours to make its exit.

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:42.110
And if there's a thick
coating of bacteria on the cyst,

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:45.650
it may even take the worms several days.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:48.970
And from there well,
it is a life of suction,

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:52.550
feeding and chain
link clones and regenerating.

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:57.030
Perhaps not normal to us,
but what's normal anyway?

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:58.912
Especially when you're a worm.

00:19:01.061 --> 00:19:05.049
Now that we’ve seen a few worms, have you
ever wondered what it would be like

00:19:05.049 --> 00:19:06.956
to live inside of one?

00:19:06.956 --> 00:19:08.936
Because I certainly haven’t.

00:19:08.936 --> 00:19:14.620
But there is a ciliate that does like to live
in the guts of worms, so in our next video,

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:18.799
we’re going to learn more about why they
do that.

00:19:26.246 --> 00:19:30.880
You’ve heard some worm horror stories, right?
We were looking some up just for this episode

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:34.010
and came across a recent headline
from ArsTechnica&nbsp;that read,

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:40.840
“Army of worm larvae hatch from man’s
bum, visibly slither under his skin,”.

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:45.900
And if that’s not enough to terrify you,
and make you feel very uncomfortable

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:50.720
there’s always the stories of painful stomach
cramps or diarrhea or nausea that eventually

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:56.150
turns out to be caused by some worms that
have taken up residence in someone’s intestines.

00:19:56.150 --> 00:20:01.799
It is terrifying and wild to think of something
so much smaller than us causing so much havoc.

00:20:01.799 --> 00:20:07.000
So, as we watch the cilia lining a worm’s
gut beat its own soothing pattern, wouldn’t

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:08.440
it feel&nbsp;like,

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:14.090
almost like, a little bit of justice if this
sight wasn’t so peaceful? If worms had to

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:17.909
worry about their own guts being taken over
by a parasite?

00:20:17.909 --> 00:20:22.731
If you’ve found yourself in this position,
seeking schadenfreude over a worm, well we

00:20:22.731 --> 00:20:27.700
have some good news for you. The worm you
see in the middle of this tank is currently

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:31.458
hosting this strange fellow, called a Radiophyra.

00:20:33.704 --> 00:20:35.704
James, our master of microscopes, had been

00:20:35.704 --> 00:20:41.910
on the hunt for the Radiophyra after seeing
this: two radiophyras linked together in a

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:47.740
chain as one divided to make more copies of
itself. It had come from the inside of one

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:52.620
of the worms he’d been watching, when he’d
accidentally squeezed a worm a bit too hard

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:57.710
under the coverslip and caused the ciliate
to pop out.

00:20:57.710 --> 00:21:03.610
Radiophyra belong to a general group of ciliates
called Astomes, or astomatid ciliates. We’ve

00:21:03.610 --> 00:21:07.090
talked about ciliates a lot on our channel,
which means that if you’ve been watching

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:11.549
us for a while, you may have picked up on
the fact that from time to time, we have said

00:21:11.549 --> 00:21:17.890
that most ciliates have an oral groove, that
opening lined with cilia that sweep bacteria

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:23.461
and algae and other tiny bits of food into
the organism. We’ve seen that oral groove

00:21:23.461 --> 00:21:29.720
at work in ciliates like stentors and paramecium,
functioning as the ciliate equivalent of a

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:32.909
mouth.
But as we have always said most ciliates,

00:21:32.909 --> 00:21:35.779
you
will have inferred, that this does not mean

00:21:35.779 --> 00:21:36.870
all ciliates.

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:40.080
And if you are looking for an exception
to the rule,

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:47.230
astomes are that exception. Astomatid ciliates
are diverse, but they are unified by one shared

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:53.190
feature, or rather, they are unified by their
lack of one shared feature, a mouth.

00:21:53.190 --> 00:21:59.547
And they don’t need a mouth because they
have something even better. They have worms.

00:22:03.234 --> 00:22:09.611
Astomatid ciliates do parasitize animals other
than worms. Some live inside mollusks, others

00:22:09.611 --> 00:22:14.919
inside leeches or even in amphibians. But
they are most commonly associated with the

00:22:14.919 --> 00:22:21.070
guts of annelid worms. So when James found
his Radiophyra, he decided to see if he could

00:22:21.070 --> 00:22:26.470
find more of them in the other worms that
were in his samples. And that meant that our

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:32.710
master of microscopes had to become a master
of worm surgery, dissecting them so he could

00:22:32.710 --> 00:22:37.730
draw out the ciliates living within.
In–side this particular aquatic worm were

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:43.620
these astomatid ciliates. From a distance,
they also look like worms.

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:48.980
But as you get closer…
And closer, their shape becomes more definite

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:55.909
except for the massive amounts of fluff around
them, a dense cloud of cilia beating away.

00:22:55.909 --> 00:23:01.169
Unfortunately, there isn’t a lot of research
on this ciliate. In fact, there isn’t a

00:23:01.169 --> 00:23:06.620
lot of research on astomatid ciliates in general.
They just aren’t destructive enough or common

00:23:06.620 --> 00:23:12.450
enough to have become either a necessary or
convenient research subject. In fact, it’s

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:18.580
not even clear whether or not we should call
them parasites. Modern day papers will sometimes

00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:25.020
refer to them endosymbionts instead, because
we don't know a lot about whether astomatid

00:23:25.020 --> 00:23:30.210
ciliates are doing much to their worm hosts,
bad or good.

00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:35.950
But the worm gut does plenty for the astomatid
ciliates. At one point in time, the ancestors

00:23:35.950 --> 00:23:41.409
of these ciliates did have mouths. But as
they found their way into worms, and specifically

00:23:41.409 --> 00:23:46.549
their guts, those oral grooves became less
and less necessary.

00:23:46.549 --> 00:23:53.070
Instead, the ciliates could rely on a form
of feeding called osmotrophy, where they simply

00:23:53.070 --> 00:23:59.590
absorb nutrients from their surroundings through
osmosis. Instead of taking in larger bits

00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:04.310
of food through their mouths and breaking
it down themselves, astomatid ciliates could

00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:10.860
just take advantage of the worm’s digestive
system to do all that breaking down for them.

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:14.419
As the worm’s
digestive enzymes&nbsp;break down complex molecules

00:24:14.419 --> 00:24:17.110
into
simpler forms&nbsp; that can travel through their

00:24:17.110 --> 00:24:21.850
own intestinal walls,
some of those nutrients would just go feed

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:26.720
the astomatid ciliate instead.
These ciliates will actually sometimes be

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:32.320
picky about making sure they’re in a particular
spot within their host’s intestinal tract.

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:37.900
And once they’ve found the right spot, the
ciliates hold themselves in place with organelles

00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:44.539
that range in shape, some use hooks, other
spines, or maybe even suckers. The flat shape

00:24:44.539 --> 00:24:49.409
of the ciliates helps them stay pressed to
the epithelium of the intestines. So while

00:24:49.409 --> 00:24:54.370
these ciliates may not need a mouth anymore,
they have found other traits necessary to

00:24:54.370 --> 00:24:59.029
their survival.
Astomatid ciliates are found in hosts from

00:24:59.029 --> 00:25:05.340
all sorts of environments. Some live in soil.
Some live in ponds. Some even live in ocean

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:11.010
waters. And scientists are using the general
tools available now to try and piece together

00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:16.580
how host and endosymbiont have shaped each
other. We can see some of that intertwined

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:22.250
story in the ciliate’s mouthless-ness, but
the specifics of that evolutionary change

00:25:22.250 --> 00:25:28.450
are laid out in their DNA, as are the other
more hidden parts of that shared history.

00:25:28.450 --> 00:25:34.600
Now we cannot know whether worms have any
feelings about their guts being home to another

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:39.330
organism. They likely don’t have much choice
in the matter, and they also likely aren’t

00:25:39.330 --> 00:25:45.559
prone to emotions like resentment the way
we might resent a worm parasitizing our bodies.

00:25:45.559 --> 00:25:49.750
But if they were to have any feelings on the
matter, we want to offer this one last detail

00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:57.179
about astomatid ciliates: many of them have
their own endosymbionts as well, bacteria

00:25:57.179 --> 00:26:03.600
that live inside them with perhaps their own
history tangled up with their ciliate host’s

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:09.030
story as well. Now we couldn’t find those
bacteria in our parasitic friends, but we

00:26:09.030 --> 00:26:15.490
like the idea that somewhere out there are
these nesting dolls of endosymbiosis: an organism

00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:22.941
that is an organism but an organism that is
also a home buried within other homes.

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:32.484
When we say that worms are everywhere, we
mean it.

00:26:32.484 --> 00:26:38.462
Even in the world of science, worms are widespread
with researchers turning to the nematode Caenorhabditis

00:26:38.462 --> 00:26:42.676
elegans as a model to study all sorts of questions.

00:26:42.676 --> 00:26:47.909
And in the process, scientists have written
some lovely things about the nematodes that

00:26:47.909 --> 00:26:49.490
make their work possible.

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:56.179
So in our next video, we’ll pay tribute
to a worm that has shaped our world in so

00:26:56.179 --> 00:26:57.775
many ways.

00:27:03.684 --> 00:27:10.409
When it comes to the muses of the animal kingdom,
the nematode seems like an unlikely well of

00:27:10.409 --> 00:27:11.409
inspiration.

00:27:11.409 --> 00:27:17.000
There may be poems about tigers burning bright,
and epic fantasy tales featuring giant birds.

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:22.930
But nematodes have two obvious factors working
against them achieving that kind of mythical

00:27:22.930 --> 00:27:24.020
status.

00:27:24.020 --> 00:27:26.559
First: they are worms.

00:27:26.559 --> 00:27:28.710
And let's not generalize too broadly, but
their

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:31.649
simple tubular bodies are just not what most

00:27:31.649 --> 00:27:35.809
people think of when it comes to the beauty
of the natural world.

00:27:35.809 --> 00:27:40.980
Second: most of them are really tiny, their
length measured in millimeters.

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:46.529
It’s hard to be inspired by an animal that
is likely to escape one’s notice entirely.

00:27:46.529 --> 00:27:53.019
Though we should note that there are nematodes
that can grow quite long—the largest discovered

00:27:53.019 --> 00:28:00.880
was found in the placenta of a sperm whale,
and it measured between 8-9 meters in length.

00:28:00.880 --> 00:28:05.545
But even that seems more likely to inspire
more nightmares than art.

00:28:07.108 --> 00:28:13.950
In 1914, a scientist named Nathan Cobb wrote
the following about nematodes:

00:28:13.950 --> 00:28:18.560
Nematodes do not furnish hides, horns, tallow,
or wool.

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:22.929
They are not fit for food, they do not produce
anything fit to eat;

00:28:22.929 --> 00:28:27.340
neither do they sing or amuse us in any way;
nor are they ornamental

00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:33.890
—in fact, when they are displayed in museums
the public votes them hideous.

00:28:33.890 --> 00:28:39.059
Judging from that quote, Cobb seems to understand
the common consensus on nematodes,

00:28:39.059 --> 00:28:44.580
which is that if there is to be a consensus,
it is not a positive one.

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:51.281
But Cobb wrote this as part of a 34 page paper
on nematodes titled “Nematodes and their

00:28:51.281 --> 00:28:52.919
Relationships,”

00:28:52.919 --> 00:28:57.929
which wonderfully documents
his fascination with the worm and argues for

00:28:57.929 --> 00:29:00.970
their importance to our understanding of the
world.

00:29:00.970 --> 00:29:04.740
So clearly, he found something inspiring about
the nematode.

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:09.510
But there is another quote from this research
paper that you may have even heard before,

00:29:09.510 --> 00:29:11.880
it is pretty famous in nematode circles.

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:15.169
And The quote is as follows:

00:29:15.169 --> 00:29:21.010
In short, if all the matter in the universe
except the nematodes were swept away,

00:29:21.010 --> 00:29:27.710
our world would still be dimly recognizable,
and if, as disembodied spirits, we could then

00:29:27.710 --> 00:29:35.299
investigate it, we should find its mountains,
hills, vales, rivers, lakes, and oceans represented

00:29:35.299 --> 00:29:39.260
by a film of nematodes.

00:29:39.260 --> 00:29:43.760
Cobb paints a world overrun by nematodes,
an image

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:47.149
created by his own experience with studying
them.

00:29:47.149 --> 00:29:50.529
But when he wrote this, he likely didn’t
have the means

00:29:50.529 --> 00:29:54.930
to estimate just how many nematodes there
are in the world.

00:29:54.930 --> 00:30:03.039
But in 2019, researchers estimated that there
are about 57 billion nematodes in the world

00:30:03.039 --> 00:30:06.340
for every human in the world.

00:30:06.340 --> 00:30:10.870
In addition, their total biomass is about
300 million tons.

00:30:10.870 --> 00:30:13.789
And that’s just for the soil nematodes.

00:30:13.789 --> 00:30:18.360
When you take into consideration nematodes
living in freshwater or marine habitats or

00:30:18.360 --> 00:30:23.510
inside of other animals, the numbers only
go up from there.

00:30:23.510 --> 00:30:28.649
Nematodes are considered to be one of the
most abundant animals on earth.

00:30:28.649 --> 00:30:32.570
But the beauty of Cobb’s writing on their
abundance

00:30:32.570 --> 00:30:36.580
is that he doesn’t just capture the notion
of the numbers.

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:42.370
The world he describes isn’t just a mass
of worms that we’ve been allowed space inside

00:30:42.370 --> 00:30:43.370
of.

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:49.730
The world he describes—the one that we live
in—has its forms and landscapes sculpted

00:30:49.730 --> 00:30:58.850
and cultivated by worms, their bodies shaped
around and inside trees and plants and animals.

00:30:58.850 --> 00:31:02.370
Of course, not all of those links between
nematodes

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:05.309
and other organisms are always so pleasant.

00:31:05.309 --> 00:31:11.429
In “Nematodes and their Relationships,”
Cobb mentions that he once removed over 40,000

00:31:11.429 --> 00:31:13.750
nematodes from the stomach of a wallaby.

00:31:13.750 --> 00:31:18.600
And if that isn’t daunting enough to imagine,
there are always the ancient texts that date

00:31:18.600 --> 00:31:24.580
back thousands of years, documenting intestinal
worms found in people.

00:31:24.580 --> 00:31:26.840
There are also the nematode eggs that have
been

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:29.679
found preserved in mummies and in fossilized
poop.

00:31:29.679 --> 00:31:36.600
In the 19th century, scientists were able
to show that the parasite Ascaris lumbricoides

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:39.210
which resides in the intestines and produces
eggs

00:31:39.210 --> 00:31:40.880
that are passed on through poop

00:31:40.880 --> 00:31:44.260
finds its way into people who have ingested
their eggs

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:46.269
on contaminated food.

00:31:46.269 --> 00:31:51.899
To prove this, a scientist named Giovanni
Battista Grassi infected himself with their

00:31:51.899 --> 00:31:55.850
eggs and then hunted for those eggs in his
feces.

00:31:55.850 --> 00:32:01.299
A peculiar aside here this is not the first
time we’ve described a scientist hunting

00:32:01.299 --> 00:32:04.639
through his own poop for evidence of the microcosmos

00:32:04.639 --> 00:32:11.260
you can watch our Leeuwenhoek episode
for more tales of this type.

00:32:11.260 --> 00:32:14.450
Now there are plenty of other
nematode parasites that have

00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:18.340
their own unique life cycles and horrifying
effects.

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:22.320
Some worms are transmitted through the soil,
others through insects.

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:27.230
Some inflame the limbs, others trigger massive
blood loss.

00:32:27.230 --> 00:32:32.480
Nematodes can also parasitize plants, attacking
the roots or stem or flowers,

00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:36.029
sometimes destroying entire crops in the process.

00:32:36.029 --> 00:32:43.409
And so with such a destructive path, the nematode
feels like an enemy, something we study only

00:32:43.409 --> 00:32:46.620
to understand how we can fight it.

00:32:46.620 --> 00:32:50.789
But Cobb asks a simple question in his writing:

00:32:50.789 --> 00:32:56.389
What would be our conception of the insect
group as a whole if our knowledge was largely

00:32:56.389 --> 00:33:00.990
confined to these simple and
degenerate parasitic forms?

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:05.809
In the case of insects, we have enough experience
with them to know that whatever squeamishness

00:33:05.809 --> 00:33:10.240
they inspire, they are also integral parts
of our world.

00:33:10.240 --> 00:33:14.130
And likewise most nematodes are actually free-living
species.

00:33:14.130 --> 00:33:19.600
It’s just that the parasites have come to
dominate both our imagination and our knowledge

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:23.510
because of their proximity and their consequences
for our health.

00:33:23.510 --> 00:33:29.190
But those massively abundant soil nematodes
they are vital to our world,

00:33:29.190 --> 00:33:31.970
feeding on bacteria and fungi.

00:33:31.970 --> 00:33:36.889
In the process, they release nitrogen back
into the soil, sustaining other organisms

00:33:36.889 --> 00:33:38.951
and plants that may use it.

00:33:40.741 --> 00:33:47.390
A healthy soil is full of these free-living
nematodes, whose presence reflects the overall

00:33:47.390 --> 00:33:50.110
diversity of microbes around them.

00:33:50.110 --> 00:33:56.740
The information is all there, it is up to
us to uncover their secrets.

00:33:56.740 --> 00:34:02.460
Perhaps this why Cobb ended “Nematodes and
their relationships” with this call to arms

00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:05.898
for the field of nematology:

00:34:05.898 --> 00:34:12.599
They offer an exceptional field of study;
and probably constitute among the last great

00:34:12.599 --> 00:34:20.690
organic group worthy of a separate branch
of biological science comparable with entomology—

00:34:20.690 --> 00:34:22.030
nematology.

00:34:22.030 --> 00:34:29.770
We can’t speak to any claims that the nematode
is the quote “last great organic group”

00:34:29.770 --> 00:34:31.710
worthy of its own branch of study.

00:34:31.710 --> 00:34:37.480
But we do feel that he has been vindicated
by the fact that one of the most popular model

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:41.490
organisms used today is a free-living nematode:

00:34:41.490 --> 00:34:46.149
Caenorhabditis elegans, better known as C.
elegans.

00:34:46.149 --> 00:34:50.179
While the worm is found in soils all over
the world, what it’s perhaps best known

00:34:50.179 --> 00:34:53.980
for is the life it lives in labs.

00:34:53.980 --> 00:34:59.700
While scientists had described various aspects
of C. elegans life early in the 20th century,

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:06.260
it wasn’t until 1965 that the scientist
Sydney Brenner turned to the worm as a model

00:35:06.260 --> 00:35:10.600
organism, relying on its fast life cycle,
small size,

00:35:10.600 --> 00:35:15.950
and capability to produce more
than 1,000 eggs a day.

00:35:15.950 --> 00:35:20.640
There were those who thought the worm’s
simplicity would render it useless in studies

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:22.890
of morphology and behavior.

00:35:22.890 --> 00:35:28.200
But time and technology has turned C. elegans
into a molecular muse.

00:35:28.200 --> 00:35:34.610
It has taught us about how life develops,
how human diseases progress, and how cells

00:35:34.610 --> 00:35:35.940
die.

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:41.440
When Cobb wrote, “My experience in this
matter makes me very confident in saying that

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:46.880
professors of biology could do far worse than
to introduce into their courses a more careful

00:35:46.880 --> 00:35:52.430
examination of these creatures,” he didn’t
know that one day, many of those professors

00:35:52.430 --> 00:35:56.109
of biology wouldn’t just be introducing
nematodes into their courses.

00:35:56.109 --> 00:36:00.869
They would be introducing them into their
labs.

00:36:00.869 --> 00:36:07.130
When Cobb died in 1932, scientists didn’t
even know how genetic information was encoded

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:08.130
in DNA.

00:36:08.130 --> 00:36:13.420
More than 50 years later, C. elegans became
the first multicellular organism

00:36:13.420 --> 00:36:16.330
to have its genome sequenced.

00:36:16.330 --> 00:36:21.619
And as it continues to teach us about our
world, NASA has even sent them to space

00:36:21.619 --> 00:36:25.420
so we can learn about how life ages outside
of this planet.

00:36:25.420 --> 00:36:29.160
So maybe the nematode is not the muse we expected.

00:36:29.160 --> 00:36:33.849
But over the past century, they’ve become
one nonetheless.

00:36:33.849 --> 00:36:41.069
And perhaps that is the most fitting testament
to what a muse often actually is, a surprising

00:36:41.069 --> 00:36:47.990
source of inspiration that seems to come from
nowhere, and then permeates until you see

00:36:47.990 --> 00:36:49.986
it everywhere.

00:36:53.404 --> 00:36:58.349
We’ve been on a journey in this compilation
through knowledge and a growing appreciation

00:36:58.349 --> 00:36:59.349
for worms.

00:36:59.349 --> 00:37:05.280
And it brings us to our last video, where
we went from not just being resigned to the

00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:11.690
presence of worms, but actively seeking one
out and dealing with all the surprising challenges

00:37:11.690 --> 00:37:12.764
along the way.

00:37:20.186 --> 00:37:24.560
We have spent most of our journey through
the microcosmos seeking out the organisms

00:37:24.560 --> 00:37:30.220
that are too small to see with the human eye.
The bacteria, the ciliates, the tardigrades.

00:37:30.220 --> 00:37:35.640
And Part of what makes them so exciting to
find is that they are so tiny. Every moment

00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:40.589
we spend with one of these organisms is a
peek into something exceptional in our experience

00:37:40.589 --> 00:37:46.190
of the world, and it’s the result of how
much work James, our master of microscopes,

00:37:46.190 --> 00:37:50.050
has to put into hunting down as many microbes
as he can.

00:37:50.050 --> 00:37:54.950
And sometimes, that effort requires a lot
of persistence.

00:37:54.950 --> 00:38:03.140
Take the creature we’re going to focus on
today: the bristle worm. This has been one

00:38:03.140 --> 00:38:08.040
of the white whales for our channel for some
time. And as you watch it, you can perhaps

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:14.640
understand why we have been searching so hard
for it. It’s got the body of a pipe cleaner

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:16.920
with the head of a cartoon dragon.

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:21.820
And maybe you also understand that just because
we’ve been wanting to find one of these

00:38:21.820 --> 00:38:27.270
worms, that doesn’t mean we are guaranteed
anything. After all, one of the things you

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:30.870
have to accept about the microcosmos, and
microbe hunting,

00:38:30.870 --> 00:38:37.510
is that it is a big world full of tiny creatures,
and it can take a while to find some of them.

00:38:37.510 --> 00:38:41.440
The fact that we are showing you one of those
bristle worms right now spoils the twist we

00:38:41.440 --> 00:38:46.650
would usually build into, but, surprise, we
found a bristle worm!

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:51.530
The real twist is that this was not the first
bristleworm we found.

00:38:51.530 --> 00:38:56.960
This was the first bristle worm we found.
Twenty centimeters of segments and bristles

00:38:56.960 --> 00:39:02.099
climbing up the side of a tank before burrowing
back beneath the sand.

00:39:02.099 --> 00:39:06.280
Unfortunately, that’s just about all the
video we got of that bristle worm.

00:39:06.280 --> 00:39:09.710
James spent so much time trying to find this
bristle worm again that he

00:39:09.710 --> 00:39:15.400
started to feel a connection to it. So he
decided to give it a name: Gunther.

00:39:15.400 --> 00:39:21.450
James spent hours trying to catch it without
hurting the worm. But Gunther has hundreds

00:39:21.450 --> 00:39:26.300
of appendages that can grab onto sand, and
James didn’t want to accidentally snap the

00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:30.819
worm in half with his tweezers. So instead
of showing all the features of Gunther that

00:39:30.819 --> 00:39:36.390
we wanted to show, we’re going to show clips
of this bristle worm instead because it kind

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:40.490
of resembles Gunther, except a lot tinier.

00:39:40.490 --> 00:39:46.130
Gunther is hopefully still somewhere in that
tank, living a nice life in the burrow it

00:39:46.130 --> 00:39:51.410
dug for itself. But it’s hard to know exactly
where in the tank Gunther made its home because

00:39:51.410 --> 00:39:56.160
it would only show activity in the dark. The
moment James tried to turn the lights on and

00:39:56.160 --> 00:40:00.900
watch the worm in motion, it would vanish
again under the sand. And as it became harder

00:40:00.900 --> 00:40:06.359
and harder to find, James kept hoping that
one day, something would bring another bristle

00:40:06.359 --> 00:40:08.070
worm to him.

00:40:08.070 --> 00:40:13.550
And then, one day, James received a package
from a coral farm containing sediments and

00:40:13.550 --> 00:40:18.460
other things that might contain some interesting
organisms. We talked about some of those organisms

00:40:18.460 --> 00:40:20.900
that James found in our last episode.

00:40:20.900 --> 00:40:27.030
But in addition to all of those organisms,
James found several species of his long sought

00:40:27.030 --> 00:40:32.720
after bristle worm.

00:40:32.720 --> 00:40:37.640
Bristle worms are also known as polychaetes,
and they’re part of the segmented worm phylum

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:39.410
known as Annelids.

00:40:39.410 --> 00:40:44.820
The name “polychaete” translates in Greek
to “many hairs.” Those stiff hairs are

00:40:44.820 --> 00:40:49.380
called setae, and for most polychaetes, they’re
attached to paddle-like appendages called

00:40:49.380 --> 00:40:53.700
the parapodia that branch off each segment
of the worm.

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:59.470
Our giant friend Gunther most likely belongs
to the order Eunicida, but its microscopic

00:40:59.470 --> 00:41:05.390
look-alike is more unknown to us. But we can
imagine that it spends its life crawling around

00:41:05.390 --> 00:41:10.079
the sand and feeding on algae, or whatever
else it can take a bite out of.

00:41:10.079 --> 00:41:15.609
You can see this one moving its mouth in slow
motion, like a weird pair of pincers inside

00:41:15.609 --> 00:41:22.380
clamping down on something, and the movement
is even more dramatic in full speed.

00:41:22.380 --> 00:41:27.540
This probably looks like a bunch of tiny individual
orange worms tangled together. But it is,

00:41:27.540 --> 00:41:34.910
in fact, a worm belonging to the genus Cirratulus
that can get to around 12 centimeters in length.

00:41:34.910 --> 00:41:39.410
And along with its distinctive color, the
worm is easy to spot in wet sandy mud because

00:41:39.410 --> 00:41:45.390
of those threads you see waving across your
screen. Some of these threads are tentacles,

00:41:45.390 --> 00:41:48.240
but others are actually gills.

00:41:48.240 --> 00:41:54.319
And in the water, those threads seem to float
serenely. But this effect is lost on land.

00:41:54.319 --> 00:41:59.750
In a paper from the beginning of the 20th
century titled “Notes on the Ecology of

00:41:59.750 --> 00:42:05.990
Cirratulus tentaculatus,” the author wrote,
“When withdrawn from the mud Cirratulus

00:42:05.990 --> 00:42:12.470
presents an exceeding limp and bedraggled
appearance.”

00:42:12.470 --> 00:42:18.339
This worm has a more imposing appearance but
also a funnier name. It belongs to the terebellid

00:42:18.339 --> 00:42:23.270
order, but it’s known as a spaghetti worm.
These tropical worms live in sand, building

00:42:23.270 --> 00:42:29.630
tubes out of gravel and limestone to live
in. Their tentacles spill out from the tube,

00:42:29.630 --> 00:42:35.500
sometimes extending as far as a meter to gather
building materials and food for the worm.

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:40.369
To reproduce, spaghetti worms will release
their eggs and sperm into the water, but only

00:42:40.369 --> 00:42:46.200
at night and sometimes even without other
males or females around. They seem to do this

00:42:46.200 --> 00:42:51.430
on a lunar cycle, with a limited two week
window for these gametes to release and find

00:42:51.430 --> 00:42:56.950
each other. That seems like an extraordinarily
chance-y way to reproduce, but it seems to

00:42:56.950 --> 00:42:59.099
work for this worm.

00:42:59.099 --> 00:43:03.550
These are the bristle worms we were able to
find from our coral farm samples. And so it

00:43:03.550 --> 00:43:08.280
seems like our hunt for this white whale might
be over.

00:43:08.280 --> 00:43:12.859
Except, the more we’ve been reading about
bristle worms, the more we wish we could find

00:43:12.859 --> 00:43:20.410
even more. Because these are an animal whose
existence seems designed to inspire lists

00:43:20.410 --> 00:43:26.790
of fun facts and bizarre trivia. There are
thousands of species of polychaetes, and they

00:43:26.790 --> 00:43:31.579
all seem to have something remarkable or weird
about them.

00:43:31.579 --> 00:43:37.010
Some spend their lives in tubes that stick
up from the sand, using their parapodia to

00:43:37.010 --> 00:43:42.119
paddle water through the burrow. Others have
managed to carve out lives near hydrothermal

00:43:42.119 --> 00:43:47.569
vents. Some sound like they’re straight
out of a horror novel, growing up to ten feet

00:43:47.569 --> 00:43:54.140
long or dining on the bones of decomposing
animals. And some even have eyes on both ends

00:43:54.140 --> 00:43:55.730
of their bodies.

00:43:55.730 --> 00:44:02.940
So knowing that all of these different species
are out there, how could we ever end our quest

00:44:02.940 --> 00:44:04.060
for the bristle worm?

00:44:10.717 --> 00:44:16.779
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:44:16.779 --> 00:44:21.441
The people whose names are on your screen
right now, they are our Patreon patrons.

00:44:21.441 --> 00:44:24.910
Thank you so much to all of them, 
and if you want to become a patron

00:44:24.910 --> 00:44:27.963
you can go to Patreon.com/JounreytoMicro.

00:44:27.963 --> 00:44:31.990
If you want to see more from our master of
microscopes, James Weiss, and why wouldn’t

00:44:31.990 --> 00:44:36.619
you, check out Jam &amp; Germs on Instagram, and
if you want to see more from us, there is

00:44:36.619 --> 00:44:39.536
always a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

